## ABSTRACT
The principle of reticular chemistry has been widely used to guide the design of crystalline porous materials such as metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs). While in the early strategies only the symmetries of the building blocks were considered for reticular synthesis of COFs, recently a few researches on COFs with hierarchical porosities indicate that connecting sequence of building blocks also plays a crucial role in determining crystalline structures of COFs. However, this important phenomenon has not been systematically investigated yet. In this article, a model system has been established to demonstrate how different connecting sequences of two $C_{2v}$ symmetric building blocks lead to the formation of four two-dimensional (2D) COFs with distinct framework structures. To verify this concept, target synthesis was conducted to produce three COFs, whose structures were confirmed by powder $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction and pore size distribution analysis.
## KEYWORDS
reticular chemistry, covalent organic framework, connecting sequence, building block
## Introduction
Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are a type of porous crystalline polymers formed by linking rigid organic building blocks with strong covalent bonds to produce well-ordered two-dimensional (2D) or three-dimensional (3D) network structures [1–4]. As an emerging class of organic porous materials, COFs have attracted immerse attention due to their versatile applications in many fields such as gas storage and separation [5–8], catalysis [9–12], sensing [13–16], drug delivery [17–20], energy storage [21–25], water splitting [26–30], photoelectric devices [31–34], and environmental remediation [35–38]. In the implementation of these applications, pore textures, which are defined by framework structures of COFs, play a crucial role as they make a significant impact on the properties of COFs. Generally, the design strategy for the framework structure of a COF follows the principle of reticular chemistry [39]. For example, COFs consisting of uniform hexagonal pores can be constructed from the condensation of a $C_{3}$ -symmetric monomer with a $C_{3}$ - or $C_{2}$ -symmetric one [40–47], while the assembly of a $C_{4}$ -symmetric building block with a linear one gives rise to a COF with tetragonal lattice [48–51]. Directed by such principle, a lot of COFs have been designed and synthesized over the past decade [52–54]. And for the building blocks, only the combination of different symmetries is needed to be considered for reticulating the framework structures. However, such stereotype was broken recently by several examples reported by our [55–57] and Zhang’s [58] groups, which demonstrated that the alteration of connecting sequence of the building blocks could lead to the formation of 2D COFs with different framework structures. This new discovery strongly suggests that reticulating the structure of a COF needs not only to pay attention to the symmetries of the building blocks, but also to consider their connecting sequence, especially for the building blocks with low symmetries. However, so far such important phenomenon has just been sparsely observed and it has not been given a detailed investigation [59, 60]. Considering the crucial role the connecting sequence playing in reticular chemistry, a systematic study to illustrate how it exerts an influence on the formation of framework structures is highly desired. In this work, we have established a model system to demonstrate changes of the connecting sequence of building blocks with the same symmetry resulting in the output of four 2D COFs with different framework structures. Three COFs have been synthesized through the condensations of the monomers with different chemical structures but the same symmetry.
## 2 Results and discussion
The model system was designed based on the combination of two $C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric building blocks. As shown in Scheme 1, the assembly of two $C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric building blocks would result in four different types of framework structures depending on their connecting sequences which include two aspects, bonding and orientation. The connection of 1 and a (marked as 1-a) and the connection of 1 and b (marked as 1-b) give two different kinds of intermediate units A and B, respectively. Next, for intermediate A, the further connections through 1-a and 2-b lead to the formation of a single-pore (SP) 2D COF holding uniform rhombus-like pores (SP-r) and the formation of a 2D COF bearing two different kinds of pores (dual-pore, DP), respectively. The difference between them lies in the distinct orientation of the building blocks, which makes a difference on the bonding sequences. On the other hand, for the intermediate B formed from the connection of 1-b, the following connections via $^{1-\mathrm{b},}$ 2-a and $2{\cdot}\mathrm{b}$ give rise to a COF with uniform hexagonal pores (SP-h) and a COF consisting of three different kinds of pores (triple-pore, TP), respectively, depending on the orientation of the building blocks. The latter is the most complicated 2D COF structure that can be produced by assembly of the two
$C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric building blocks.
To verify the above model system, target synthesis was conducted. Experimentally, condensations of monomers with different structures but the same symmetry were implemented. In this context, three $C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric monomers, [[1,1':4',1''- terphenyl]- ${}_{3,4",5}$ -tricarbaldehyde (TP3TCA), [1,1':3',1''-terphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -tricarbaldehyde (TP4TCA) and [1,1':4',1'':3'', 1''':4''',1''''- quinquephenyl]- $4{,}4^{\prime\prime\prime}{,}5^{\prime\prime}$ -tricarbaldehyde (QPTCA) were designed and synthesized (Scheme 2). 1,1':3',1''-terphenyl]- 4,4'',5'-triamine (TPTA) was selected as another $C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric monomer to condense with the trialdehydes, respectively, to afford COFs under solvothermal conditions. In order to produce COFs with high crystallinity, the condensation conditions were optimized, for which a series of conditions were screened by varying the solvent, temperature and concentration of acetic acid. For the COF prepared from TPTA and TP3TCA (the product was named COF-TPTA-1), a mixture of o-dichlorobenzene/n-butanol/acetic acid (aq., 6 M) $(10/1/1.1,\mathrm{v/v/v})$ is the best among all the solvent systems screened. In the case of the COF produced from the condensation of TPTA and TP4TCA (the product was named COF-TPTA-2), the types of solvents are same, but the ratio changes to $1/10/1.1\mathrm{(v/v/v)}$ for o-dichlorobenzene/ n-butanol/acetic acid (aq., 9 M). The third COF, COF-TPTA-3, was obtained from the condensation of TPTA and QPTCA, in a mixture of o-dichlorobenzene, $\mathfrak{n}$ -butanol, and acetic acid (aq., 6 M) with a ratio of $3/1/0.4\ \mathrm{(v/v/v)}$ . These results reveal a significant influence of solvent system on the crystallinity of the COFs. The condensation reactions were carried out in sealed glass ampules at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ to afford COF crystallites as yellow powders.
Structural simulations were performed using Materials Studio version 7.0, from which the theoretical crystal structures of the four types of COFs with both eclipsed (AA) and staggered (AB) stacking manners were modelled after geometry optimizations by the Material Studio Forcite molecular dynamics module method. The as-obtained crystallites were submitted to powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis to elucidate their crystal structures. For COF-TPTA-1, as shown in Fig. 1, a strong peak corresponding to (100) diffraction is clearly observed at $2\theta=$ $3.95^{\mathrm{o}}$ . In addition to this peak, diffraction peaks at $5.94^{\mathrm{o}}$ , $7.90^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $26.24^{\mathrm{o}}$ are also observed, which are assignable to the diffractions of (110), (200) and (001) facets, respectively. As noted above, the condensation of TPTA and TP3TCA theoretically could give rise to four different types of COFs, that is, TP, DP, $\operatorname{SP-r}$ and SP-h. The PXRD patterns of their simulated crystal structures with eclipsed stacking are provided as Figs. 1(c)–1(f). A close comparison between them and the experimental pattern indicates that the experimental PXRD pattern of COF-TPTA-1 matches well with the simulated one predicated for the $\mathrm{{\calSP-h}}$ structure with eclipsed stacking (Fig. 1(f) and Fig. S11 in the ESM), while other structural models with AA stacking could be ruled out. Furthermore, none of the calculated PXRD patterns of the four simulated crystal structures with staggered stacking matches with the experimental data (Fig. S12 in the ESM). These results strongly suggest that the as-formed polymer should possess a framework holding uniform hexagonal pores and adopts AA stacking (SP-h in Scheme 1 and Fig. 1(j)). Pawley refinement gave the unit-cell parameters of $a=25.01$ Å, $b=$ $15.04\mathrm{\AA}$ ， $c=3.36\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=\beta=90.00^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $\gamma=116.11^{\circ}$ , with $R_{\mathrm{wp}}=$ $1.91\%$ and $R_{\mathrm{p}}=1.06\%$ . Checking the difference plot between the experimental and refined PXRD patterns, we found that they matched each other well (Fig. 1(b)).
Similar to COF-TPTA-1, the crystal structure of COF-TPTA-2 was also elucidated by the comparison of its experimental PXRD pattern with the simulated ones (Fig. 2). A series of peaks at 4.81o, $8.35^{\mathrm{o}}$ , $9.62^{\mathrm{o}}$ , and $25.15^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ are observed, which are assignable to (110), (300), (220) and (001) reflections, respectively (Fig. 2(a)). The comparison of the simulated PXRD patterns with the experimental one reveals that COF-TPTA-2 has TP structure with AA stacking (Fig. 2 and Fig. S13 in the ESM). Notably, the formation of DP structure could be ruled out by the absence of (100) peak and the presence of (300) and (220) peaks in the experimental PXRD pattern (Fig. S14 in the ESM). The refined PXRD profile matches the experimental pattern quite well, which gives the unit cell parameters of $a=\bar{b}=36.2\bar{2}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $c=3.54\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=\beta=90.00^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $\gamma=120.00^{\circ}$ , with $R_{\mathrm{wp}}=2.68\%$ and $R_{\mathrm{p}}=1.74\%$ . Although the COF-TPTA-2 was generated from the connecting sequence which leads to the formation of a TP structure, it is noteworthy that, as a special case, it actually shows a dual-pore feature due to the same branch length of the two building blocks.
In the case of COF-TPTA-3, as illustrated in Fig. 3(a), a series of peaks at $3.86^{\mathrm{o}}$ , $7.72^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $25.26^{\mathrm{o}}$ are observed, which are assignable to (110), (220) and (001) reflections, respectively. The comparison of PXRD patterns of the four simulated structures with the experimental one reveals COF-TPTA-3 possessing TP structure with AA stacking (Fig. 3(c)). Notably, the simulated PXRD pattern of DP structure is quite similar to that of TP structure. To our delight, the DP structure could be ruled out by analysing the experimentally observed peak at $7.72^{\mathrm{o}}$ . This peak was not observed in the simulated PXRD pattern of the DP structure. Instead, it was identified in the simulated PXRD pattern of the TP structure and assignable to (220) facet (Fig. S15 in the ESM). Moreover, the predicted TP structure with AA stacking could be corroborated by pore distribution analysis (vide infra). Similarly, other structure models with AA or AB stacking could also be ruled out by the comparisons between the simulated and the experimental PXRD patterns (Figs. 3(d)–3(f) and Fig. S16 in the ESM). The refined PXRD profile matches the experimental pattern quite well, which gives the unit cell parameters of $a\overset{\cdot}{=}b=45.\overset{\cdot}{5}1\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}$ , $c=3.52\mathrm{~\AA}.$ , $\alpha=\beta=90.00^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $\gamma=120.00\mathrm{^o};$ , with $R_{\mathrm{wp}}=2.11\%$ and $R_{\mathrm{p}}=1.29\%$ .
The COFs were also investigated with transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The large domains of straight black-andwhite lattice fringes from the high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) images imply the highly crystalline nature of the three COFs. The interplanar spacings can be directly obtained from the distances between straight lattice fringes, which should correspond to the $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking distance between the adjacent layers in the COFs. All of COFs have clear lattice fringes with interplanar lattices spacing of 0.334, 0.353 and $0.347~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for COF-TPTA-1, COF- TPTA-TPTA and COF-TPTA-3, respectively (Fig. 4). The data are in good agreement with the refined $c$ values derived from the PXRDs, which are 0.336, 0.354 and $0.352{\mathrm{nm}}$ for the above COFs, respectively.
Nitrogen adsorption–desorption measurements revealed that the isotherms of the COFs fit the typical type I sorption model and display a steep nitrogen uptake in the low-pressure range $P/P_{\mathrm{0}}=0{-}0.01\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}},$ ), suggesting that all of the COFs hold permanent microporosity (Fig. 5). On the basis of their $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherm data $(P/P_{0}$ between 0.05 and 0.2), their Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas were calculated to be $1{,}217~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for COF-TPTA-1, $1{,}428\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for COF-TPTA-2, and $1{,}265~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for COF-TPTA-3 (Figs. S17–S22 in the ESM). Their total pore volumes were calculated to be $0.75~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (COF-TPTA-1), $0.69\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (COF-TPTA-2) and $0.67~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (COF-TPTA-3) at $P/P_{\mathrm{o}}=0.99$ , respectively. The pore size distributions (PSDs) of all of the COFs were estimated using nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT). One main distribution around $11.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ was observed for COF-TPTA-1 (Fig. 5(d)), which well matched with the theoretical pore sizes of the proposed single-pore COF $(11.5\mathrm{~\AA~})$ (Fig. S23 in the ESM). In the case of COF-TPTA-2, its PSD analysis revealed two main distributions around 14.1 and $16.8{\mathrm{~\AA}}.$ respectively (Fig. 5(e)). These values are consistent with its simulated pore distributions which were predicted to be ca. 15.4 and $17.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Fig. S24 in the ESM). For COF-TPTA-3, PSD analysis revealed three main distributions around 16.3, 20.1 and $27.3\mathrm{~\AA}.$ , respectively (Fig. 5(f)). The values are consistent with the simulated pore distribution of the TP structure which was predicted to be around 19.1, 20.8 and 25.1 Å (Fig. S25 in the ESM).
The experimental results showed that, in each system, only a specific COF structure was produced among the four kinds of theoretical models. The as-obtained framework should be the one with the thermodynamically energy-minimum state under the condensation condition. However, currently it is extremely hard or even impossible to identify the exact factors that lead to the lowest energy of the specific structures, for the situation is very complicated. The connecting sequences of building blocks, stacking of COF layers, solvation energy, and so on, may come into effect.
It must be pointed out that efforts were also made to synthesize the COFs with the SP-r and DP structures. However, the attempts were not successful even though more $C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric monomers of different structures were screened. It indicates the challenge in synthetic chemistry to achieve the synthesis of designed COFs. Although the synthesis of the two framework structures has not achieved, the theoretical design and experimental results obtained provide systematic evidence on the effects of connecting sequences of building blocks on the formation of framework structures.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, on the basis of the establishment of a model system in which two $C_{2\nu}$ -symmetric building blocks are combined to construct 2D COFs, the effects of connecting sequences of building blocks on framework structures have been clearly demonstrated. By changing their connecting sequences, theoretically four 2D COFs with different pore structures could be generated, which were experimentally verified by the synthesis of the COFs possessing different types of framework structures from the monomer with $C_{2\nu}$ symmetry. This study suggests that not only the symmetries of building blocks but also their connecting sequences are required to be considered in reticulating COF structures. It will deepen the understanding on reticular chemistry and thus should be helpful to guiding future design of framework-based materials, especially those with hierarchical porosities. Moreover, this work also demonstrates the generation of COFs with distinct topological structures from the monomers with the same symmetry but different chemical structures, which may offer a new avenue to increase the diversity of crystalline porous materials from the building blocks sharing the same symmetry.
